    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2018986991459672540",
      "url": "https://x.com/radarph_media/status/2018986991459672540",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/radarph_media/status/2018986991459672540",
      "text": "Movement at the Cabinet level is accelerating, and sources say the timeline is far tighter than many expected. Multiple industry insiders now say a leadership change at the Department of Tourism is imminent, with Christina Garcia Frasco set to exit and Patrick \"Pato\" Gregorio preparing to step in as Tourism Secretary.\n\nThe shift is expected to happen sooner rather than later, with conversations suggesting that only final paperwork and formal announcements remain. Within tourism circles, the transition is already being treated as a done deal.\n\nHere’s why radar sees Gregorio as a strong fit for the role. He is formally trained in tourism and built his career in hospitality management, including senior leadership roles at major properties such as The Manila Hotel and Waterfront Hotels and Resorts, where success is measured in arrivals, occupancy, and guest experience. His background places him squarely on the execution side of tourism, not just promotion.\n\nThat focus carried into his work as chair of the National Sports Tourism–Inter Agency Committee. Under that framework, four world championships in gymnastics, futsal, surfing, and volleyball last year brought thousands of tourists to the Philippines, translating events into real visitor traffic.\n\nWith pressure mounting for results, this handover signals a clear pivot toward delivery and outcomes.\n\nReport by Kenneth M. del Rosario\n\n#radarPHBusiness #radarPH",
